Z||000013||lowCG||07-02-99||12:47 AM||||Given those choices,definately the spindles for the front,springs in the rear.
Z||000014||theogre||07-02-99||01:00 AM||||For this one, a bit of explanation first..... Then a Very long answer.<p>My driving method is a blend of things taught me by: My parapalegic father who drove with hand controls. My mother and grandmother who often scared the hell out of me when they drove, and people think "road rage" is new... A car racing/truck driving/car showing uncle. Allot of "official" driving documents. A "few" Police Officers I know/Work for. A couple wrecks of my own. A large amount of auto repair work is also in this.<p>I've taken what makes sence from all of that, and use it as conditions require. ((State Farm has recently shown the same common sence thank god. Maybe we'll get some shit fixed now.))<p>1. If the car suddenly start squalking tires on turns it previously took with no complaint, most often either the air pressure is screwy, or the tires are worn out. With todays blended tread compound, a tire typically changes it's "stick" as tread wears down, even if tread is "legal". I automatically replace tires at 1/3 original tread, even if they are still leagal. This of course assumes that the wheels are also in good alignment. If you have any doubt of alignment, get it checked by the best shop you can find. ((I prefer Bear machines run by well trained operators. If the operator can't/won't openly discuss his training then find someplace else.)) Get a good dial type or digital air gauge. A 1-2 PSI low tire can make a huge dif in handling. Fiero spec is 30 front/30 rear in 87. My personal driving habbits like 33 front 33 rear. DO NOT inflate a tire beyond the makers max PSI rating! In hard driving or racing a fraction of a PSI can make a huge change in performance.<p>2. There are 2(two) hand placements on the steering wheel that are globally recognized. The first, 10&2, named for clock positions, is a low fatigue position that provides good control. The second, 9&3, which most race drivers use, provides maximum leverage and other things needed in racing. Which is best for a given driver depends on allot of things. Including the steering wheel itself. The thin ring hard wheel used in most cars is hideous no matter what hand position you use. (My hands "fall asleep" in seconds on such a wheel) The thick ring wheel, like the Fiero/GM "paddle horn button" wheel, is far better. This thicker ring is usually soft, and far easier to hang on to. ((The paddle wheel has 4 spokes and the 2 horn paddles are in between the spokes, not a button in the middle of the wheel. The rim is a rellativly soft rubber.)) note that neither position is one handed. One handed driving even by "proprerly" trained drivers, even in "stick" cars, is a no no.<p>3. Fiero handling, STOCK, depends allot on exactly what model you have. There are at least 3 versions on suspensions in Fiero. (According to option code research.) there is the base option. a "heavy duty" option. and WS6 which adds thing to the heavy one. If you want to improve the Fiero, short of getting an 88, add urethane bushings to the entire front sway bar first. This will give the same effect as a stiffer bar in rubber. After that replace the cradle bushings. then all the rest. After that you might consider spring options etc.<p>4. allot of people tell you Fiero brakes stink. For racing this is true. There are a number of options. I strongly recomend a "kit" approach to this for people who aren't strong in this area. Brake mods can affect not only handling, but legal issues as well. Amature brake mods can leave you "holding the bag" if there is ever a wreck. Insurance companies do check this stuff. If a "kit" lets you down, you have legal recourse against the people who made the kit.<p>5. There are multiple transmissions available. The main diferance is in the final "dif" gearing. A lower gear gives you a quicker hole. A higher gear gives you a better top end. There are several places that can tell you what these dif gears are, including GM/Pontiac. Proper gearing for an application can often do things even engine mods can't.<p>6. Someone here said Pontiac isn't a sports car company. They might be partly right. I find it interesting the things P. learned from Fiero are now their major selling point. "Wider is better." Nearly all Pontiac cars are being spread wider than their other GM counter parts. It's a simple fact.... a large, close to square, wheel base out performs a short or narrow one.<p>7. There is allot of talk, even from me, about Fiero's "bump steer" problem. There are generally 2 solutions to this. Get an 88, or lower the car and add new upper front control arms from GM. This assumes of course the steering stablizer, and everything else, is good. That big wheel twitch you feel on rough road <b>isn't</b> normal to most cars. There are a number of Fiero design issues that cause it. There is no simple fix.<p>8. if you are racing, expect to buy tires, allot of them. Not only because you've torn them up, but because each racers tire choice is extreamly dependant on their driving method, and the type racing they are doing. SCCA races have vastly differant requirements than NHRA/IHRA drags. Each differant tire type will perform differantly. For road courses you usually want all 4 tires the same. For drag you want the fronts small, and the backs wide as hell.<p>9. to answer 87's Q., and dispel some of the S.S.S. that Low sites..... Yes. age has a tremendous effect on suspension parts. Rubber bushings continue to "rot" even if the car is parked. "Torq steer" problems are common to Fiero, most front wheel drive,  cars, and even Corvette style 4 wheel independant rigs, even urethane or solid bushings may not fix all of it. Most Front wheel drivers don't notice it, but then most front wheels don't race in the states. The simplest reason for this is that bushings give, and straight pipe rears like NASCAR "stock" rears don't, or at least not so much that you notice. There's more to it than that, but I'm starting to get tired.<p>10. Some people might tell you that 4 Cyl Fiero is slow. Sometimes, including SCCA racing, "Slow" is not a bad thing. Even in traffic, I have little issue with pulling out into traffic with my 87 4 cyl. I can easily get up to match speed by the time I hit the end of a ramp. (Match speed around here means anything from 55-75mph) In SCCA racing, or any other for that matter, consistancy is far more important than flat speed. I believe NHRA champ John Force would agree with that. He want's a fast car, but he also wants a car that will perform in a predictable mannor.<p>11. There is a measurable amount of frame flex in all Fiero setups. Low's comment about busting windscreens isn't hard to believe. Place your hand on the windscreen glass and frame and hold it there as you turn a few corners and hit a few bumps. You can often feel the glass move relative to the body. The sealing compound usually takes this up, but really stiff suspensions could exceed what the Butal can drink. For the record, I've installed more than a few windscreens, and I've never seen the flex Fiero has. It rather suprised me the first few times I felt it.<p>12. Low, Stop bogarting the "recipie" for your perfect suspension and just post it. This whole forum is for the public disclosure of Fiero data. I doubt you have something that is totaly new, but at least if there is something we've missed we'll all see it. None of us are absolute experts on any of this cars performance issues. hell, even GM/Pontiac didn't know it all. [[I've seen DuPont experimental data first hand. DuPont helped develop both the pannels and finishes for this car. They were working on the "fiero project" long after GM/Pontiac dropped it. Frankly I was stunned when in 89 & 90, I walked into a Du Pont test site and still saw Fiero body parts on the test rig... Keep in mind. Du Pont makes many of the car paints you see anywhere in the world. Their is nothing I can find better than DuPont Imron paint even after 20 years. It's well worth the price you pay for it. Only PPG, and ICI, compete in this market. And they don't have anything like Imron.]]<p>
Z||000015||lowCG||07-02-99||01:58 PM||||First off,Imron rocks,and is better than a powder coat,just make real sure it goes on smooth,or else you'll be sanding from here to eternity.Does it flex well enough to work on the Fiero's panels?<br>Can you patch the factory panels?<br>OK,I will give out what I have concieved to be the most worthy street suspension in my experience,gladly.However,I cannot,and will not be responsible for what happens as a result of your driving and/or modifications to your vehicle.Ogre,I have recieved well the comments about insurance companies,and the E-brake requirements/negligence factor in tampering with this.Now to take a long breath...<br>                Just thought I'd share some of what I've experieced after spending MANY hours swapping springs and screwing around in the shop in order to achieve a very fast,compliant fiero; something that wouldn't have a tendancy to break looose in a very high speed corner.The car handled pretty good with no springs at all except for the bump stops(!),which is what most "performance springs" I tried were like!    <br>I'm into adhesion,predictability,control,not sideways,scare your mom stuff.<br>The stock springs totally suck as I'm sure you know,the rears start at too soft of a rate,then get way too stiff.I wound up(after trying many springs) using '87 Mazda  626 front springs in the rear of the Fiero,with Citation struts(lower spring seat,removable strut inserts),spacers(3/4") on the top of the springs(way too short of a spring without),and Konis,the strut upper mountings were turned upside down as well,giving a guaranteed full travel.Toe in was 1/8" total at the rear,camber was about 1.5 degrees.I made a set of control arm bushings out of U.H.M.W.(available from quality plastics suppliers),using the sleeves that were bonded into the originals.These were a struggle to take out because they're all molded together with the rubber bushings,had to smooth out the sleeves after they were out with a sander so they didn't tear-up the plastic bushings.This helped get out the squirm that the stock bushings have much of just driving in a straight line,and helped keep the camber where you want it during manuevers.The car actually rode better with this modification and no other changes.Checked the setup for wear after a year of punishment,and they were still without any slop at all.<br>Was going to use a small(1/2")sway bar at the rear as well,never tried it though.Would have made a further improvement in the ride quality too. A 1-1/4"  (or larger) bar would be neat for autocross,but deadly for the high speed stuff.Also,I slotted the rear strut(upper)mounting holes 3/8" to the rear,used washers on top,under the nuts,and this helped adhesion a little as well.Don't use a thick rubber pad on the springs either.<br>I used Fiat urethane ones on the front,and a piece of 1/8" hard rubber mat on the rear,upper only.<p>The front end of the car got '71-'82' Cadillac upper ball joints welded to the Fiero lower control arms,racing style.These are longer and raise the spindle about 5/8" or so,lowering the car,without changing the geometry of the lower arm.It raises the upper arm,and reduces bump steer,as a bonus.I was only looking for a replacement for the very poor quality,aftermarket(or dealer) ball joints.The new ones I got were consistantly loose where the stud went into the ball! Total garbage especially on a performance car.The upper ball joints were seventies German Opel from a junkyard;very good metal quality helped these parts to last as long as they did and still be better than the new replacement Fiero ones that were from a Chevette parts bin.Think I used the whole spindle from the opel as well.(I have since learned that the Opel spindles,and upper balljoints aren't needed,that Moog upper ball joints are of decent quality)The Opel steering was a near bolt in and was next before I quit the project.Springs in the front were Nuespeed lowering springs from the front of an early eighties VWRabbit,again with custom spacers(2-1/4").This again was after many combinations of front/rear spring trials conducted on late night,empty freeways.The swaybar(original) was remounted using urethane bushings on the inner mounts,rubber,or very small diameter urethane on the outer(I used a sander to make a tiny,ball shape),and with new shorter(2-5/8")links to get the bar horizontal again after altering the ride height.Shocks were Bilsteins fronts from a 122S Volvo,mounted upside down for less unsprung wieght.<br>Wow,didn't realize how much stuff I did to that car,it was all worth it however,when one night during the later part of the project,I came across this guy out in the canyons where I'd run, driving a brand new turbo Lotus Esprit.Not only did I stick with him,I almost ran him over! And all with(obviously)more stability and confidence on the bumpy,winding roads than he could ever hope for in his car;I'd be pissed if I'd spent that much and it didn't work any better than that.<br>I'm working on my recently aquired Fiero so I can enjoy again what was the best handling car I've ever experienced.I've had many sports cars.<br>Oh,yeah,the tires I used were Yokahama A509s,205/60 in the rear,and some Continentals off of a BMW 325 ,Sport Contacts, 195/65 all on stock rims,total sleeper.  
Z||000016||Motorhead||07-02-99||02:49 PM||||Great observations Ogre. But I've found that tastes in handling characteristics are alot like tastes in woman. You've got cars like the early Porsche 911, which when it left the road, was allways rear first, to the Triumph inline 6 cylinders, which plowed a furrow through the outfield as it resisted its drivers attempts at directional changes.<p>Both of these cars have loyal followers, who would trade nothing for their car's "superior" handling.<p>I agree the Fiero's bumpsteer is unforgiving, but compared to the mid eighties Chrysler turbo Laser, torque steer is non existant. A friend owned one back then and loved this car to no end, first time I drove it, I knew this guy was suicidal!  Put your foot in it and it was like it had a mind of it's own. Very fast, but had this tendency to turn right , and if you weren't inclined to agree it would wrench the wheel from your hands.<p>Granted this was the early days of front wheel drive, but I'm sure there's someone out there that still thinks this is a great car.<p>When I think of our Fieros, I compare it to the Lotus Europa. Fell in love with it the first time I saw it! Didn't care about the shortfalls, just that sexy mid-engine styling, and that magical weight bias.  I wonder what kind of numbers that car got on the skidpad? 
Z||000017||Eric||07-02-99||05:32 PM||||I'm starting to feel left out of this suspension related topic.  I wish there were more people out there who knew the "setup" for an 88.  I'd like to put Konis and poly bushings on my car, but after that what is there to do?  I've been told you can't screw with the springs (it's a shame because the front end has a lift look to it).  As far as I know, nobody makes lowering springs for the 88s.<p>What about alignments?  I just got a professional alignment a few months ago.  I told the guy I wanted a good performance street setup (I think I had too much rear camber before because the tires wore after about 15,000 miles).  When I got it back there was no camber in the rear or maybe just 1/2 degree.  Does this really matter? for my application (sometimes I autocross, but I do it on street tires).
Z||000018||batboy||07-02-99||06:16 PM||||LowCG and some of the others know much more about the suspension stuff than I do, but I do know that there are lower sport springs available for the 88's. The Fiero store carries some, but I don't know much about them. Try this link for some hi-fi stuff: http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/suspen.htm
Z||000019||theogre||07-02-99||09:32 PM||||if the nose is up on a Fiero, it's likely that in reality the tail is dragging. This can be cause by the springs, the struts, or both. See how it sets with new struts.<p>I'm going to have to replace my struts soon..... the rear of the 87 is gettting the boingy boingies. It sit's normal, but it's getting mushy, and the tops of the struts are damp. Can't complain for over 133+ K miles
Z||000020||lowCG||07-02-99||10:21 PM||||Pretty much taken this thread on a trip to mars,sorry,but anyways,excessive rear camber will allow the tires to wear un-evenly,if driven casually,if this is what happened,your new allignment will help this.<br>I think really,though,that the rear toe-in would be more of a factor for tire wear.Don't know how you drive/how soft the tires are(were),but the camber in back felt great at well over one degree,with solid bushings in the control arms when I'd tried a bunch of setups.<br>By the way,I tried different settings(toe,camber,caster)with each set of springs/geometry changes,after first testing with un-changed settings for a fair comparison of the initial change(spring rate/geometry)throughout my escapade.<br>Did you notice a decrease in rear adhesion after your allignment?
Z||000021||SCCA FIERO||07-02-99||11:22 PM||||Eric, I think all 88s are like that. Mine is and all the GTs I see are too. Maybe they did it to keep the front from plowing up pavement like my 86 does.
Z||000022||Eric||07-03-99||09:18 AM||||LowCG, I think I may have compensated for the lack of rear adhesion by putting new 225s on the back right after I got it aligned.  I took it autocrossing with the new tires just three days after I installed them.  At low speeds, it was very hard to get the back end to break loose.  There was a 90 degree corner after a strait.  I got up to nearly 50mph and slammed on the breaks and downshifted and even then the back didn't want to break loose.  I can't really say what the car feels like at high speeds since I haven't had the nerve to try.  I can tell you, the car feels glued to the road when I go into a corner over 60mph.  It actually is easier to drive in a corner than going strait!
Z||000023||lowCG||07-03-99||09:45 AM||||Sounds fun,I guess a good way to go without prescribing numbers to situation that involves many factors,would be to just check your rear tire wear every now and then;if the inside is more worn,maybe you have more camber than you need/use.I've seen cars with probably 2 degrees of camber,and evenly worn tires(agressive driver).
Z||000024||Eric||07-03-99||06:30 PM||||Yeah, I live out in farm country so most of the time I drive in a strait line.  When I do come across a corner, everybody in a 500 yd radius knows it because they can hear my squealing tires.  I don't like the drag racing as much as cornering fast through difficult and technical turns.  The reason I crave power is so I can use my right foot more to steer.  With the 88 suspension, I guess it might be a good idea to leave well enough alone.  I can live with the higher ride height, as long as the handling is not comprimised.  The only slack worth taking up are the bushings and the shocks and in the winter I'll take care of that.  Until then I'll be putting my car through as many four wheel drifts as I see fit.  Happy sliding!
Z||000025||lowCG||07-03-99||08:27 PM||||I know what you mean about the power,it seemed that when I was finally happy with my setup,that I would always run out of beans while drifting into a corner/attempting to power out.Pretty neat,huh,when you don't need brakes,just scrub off a bunch of speed while sliding!I'm afraid to try a move like that with this old car I just got,maybe <br>after some serious<br>maintenance,adjustments,and tires.
